// Ticket 577 (F3): the plain words a new user types for a command whose title says it another way ("preferences" for Open
// settings, "shortcuts" for the keys list, "dark mode" for the theme). Key `palette.kw.<command id>`, phrases split by "|".
// The palette ranks a whole-word match above a title that only contains the word, so the command named "Settings" is not
// buried by the hundred "Settings: ..." rows. Read by i18n/lib/commandTitle.ts (commandWords). Not declared in AreaKeys:
// the keys are built from command ids and read through `has`.
//
// Ticket 609 (first-use walks 579, 603, 605, 608, each found one more dead end): EVERY command has 2-5 phrases here, in all
// 8 languages: the everyday verb and its object ("close session", "clear screen"), a common synonym, and the word a peer app
// uses for the same thing (VS Code, Orca, T3 Code, herdr: "reveal in Finder", "toggle sidebar", "kanban", "worktree"). A phrase
// that equals what the user typed ranks the row first (Palette.tsx fit 4), so a whole phrase is worth writing out. A Settings
// row's words come from `searchWords.<field>` (settings-search-words.ts) and this file adds only the rows that have none.
// Terms follow docs/i18n-terms.md (tests/i18n-glossary-terms.spec.ts); a verb is the infinitive or the short noun form.
